Z||000007||gkozlov||09-15-2000||08:13 PM||||I dont want to start a new topic so her goes---- I built my tube but then realized that my sub is 8 Ohm. Will this affect the sound any. It doesnt seem to hit as hard as my last sub which was 4 Ohm. If this is the cause how do I correct this?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sign1_3.jpg">  <BR><A HREF="http://g.kozlov.tripod.com/gkozlov.htm" TARGET=_blank>GKozlovs Fiero Site</A>||||reg||1
Z||000008||gkozlov||09-15-2000||10:04 PM||||Up<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Seanh||09-15-2000||10:15 PM||||No way to correct this, except get a 4omh sub. What amp are you useing to drive it? To run at the same power as the 4omh sub before, the 8omh sub would have to have twice the power, assuming it is capable of handling twice the power. I would just get a 4omh sub and take the 8omh back. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||jhogans||09-15-2000||10:28 PM||||Seanh is correct.  Nothing much you can do unless you are planning to use 2 8ohm subs.  If so, then you can wire the subs in parallel to get a 4 ohm load.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Conrad Sanville||09-17-2000||09:36 AM||||Impedence is little indication as to the output of the driver, allthough most amplifiers are able to output more power into low impedence loads.  Check the sound pressure level spec.  Some of these small-box-compatible drivers are incredibly inefficient even if they are rated at four ohms or less.  Also sealed enclosures can be 3db less efficient than ported or bandpass designs. There is no such thing as too much amplifier power. Speakers can handle surprising amounts of power as long as the amps aren't driven into clipping and the sound remains clear... ||||reg||1
Z||000012||86SE||09-18-2000||08:58 AM||||Ah, ha! Its that word again.....CLIPPING...<P>I heard it before but forgot what it meant.  Is it the amp that 'clips" or is it the speaker that clips.  My 700 watt sub is starting to go down hill, it makes all kinds of wierd sounds, but what does it sound like when the amp goes into clipping?  I have it hooked to the left channel at 1 OHM (used to be bridged at 1 ohm but the amp got a little hot after 15 min).<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||3
Z||000013||ThatRickGuy||09-18-2000||12:44 PM||||most Digital amps don't have much of a problem with clipping. Clipping is when the frequency get's chopped off at the peeks of the wave. Old tube amps used to do this (the begining of distortion, a good thing for guitars, bad for sterios) imagine a sin wave.<BR>   /--\        / <BR>  /    \      /<BR> /      \    /<BR>/        \--/<BR>(crude, but you get the idea)<BR>this is that wave clipped<BR>   ____<BR>  /    \      /<BR> /      \____/<P>you can make this happen on digital amps by running too much in (pre amp, or your sterio volume) to the power amp. Try leaving your sterio volume lower and turn up your amp volume. Speakers also have frequency ratings, trying to push a higher freq threw a sub will cause some clipping on those freqs. This is where cross overs come in handy. I keep my 12's at arround 200hz or less (at 200 they start clipping). Hope that helps.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||ThatRickGuy||09-18-2000||06:26 PM||||dang it. I just realized non of those attempts of drawing a wave worked... sorry.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Conrad Sanville||09-18-2000||07:55 PM||||<BR>Any amplifier that is over driven will raise hang.  It seems like the speaker is bottoming out on bass thrusts when in many cases it is the amp losing control of the speaker.  Technically the term subwoofer only should apply to a speaker that reproduces the bottom octave of bass.  20-40Hz with and active crossover and separate amp. Otherwise it should be simply called a woofer, or bass driver.<P>I am toying with some ideas for deep bass reproduction in my winter Fiero...Maybe a ducted system emanating from under the hood with a killer amp...<P>------------------<BR><BR>Radcon||||reg||1
